DATA STRUCTURES by Chitra Rajan
Data type: refers to domain of the values and operations that can be performed on these values.
Eg:Integer, character, real number, complex number, boolean, string
There are two types of definitions: Structural defn, Behavioural defn
Structural definition: Structure of fraction is +/-sign, numerator, denominator
Behavioral definition: function create_fraction(N,D) is the behaviour
Implementation of fraction in C:
Struct fraction {
  Int Num, denr
}f;
Or 
#define Nr 0
#define Dr 1
Typedef  int fraction;
Fraction f[Nr]=2, f[Dr]=3;

Abstract data types(ADT) : where members of the data type are unknown to the users of the type. Abstraction refers to defining new types and hiding out implementation of those types
Eg: CRUD operations on fractions
Advantage of ADT:
  Isolates application from implementation
  Supports modular programming
  Supports encapsulation(binding of data and member functions)
Pre condition: eg., inserting a stack assumes enough space
Post condition: effect of inserting eg printing error message if conditions are not satisfied

Data structures: A physical representation of ADT. eg stacks queue tree graph
Types of data types:
Set - students of a class
Linear - queue at ticket counter, jobs in printer queue, computer lan with ring topology
Hierarchical - family tree, organization chart, files and folders in file system
network(graph) - road map, communication network

Types of data structures:
On the basis of type of data - primitive ds(int, char, float), compound(array)
On the basis of ordering of elements - linear / non linear ds

Algorithms analysis:
An algorithm is defined as step by step to solve a problem
Types of algorithms:(DGBDynamoB)
Divide and conquer
Greedy algorithm
Backtracking
Dynamic programming
Branch and bound

Divide and conquer: dividing a problem into smaller units and each unit is solved(conquered) independently
Greedy algo: Imagine 4kg bag to be filled with 2kg almond, 2 kg stone, 10g gold, 100g silver, 2kg paper. Filling it with 10g gold only is greedy and it does not offer optimal solution of maximum profit. This is knapsack problem. 
 Filling it with 10g gold, 100g silver, 2kg almond 2.11 kg < 4kg is optimal solution. Greedy algo does not offer optimal solution
 i.e greedy algo offers local optima solution, instead of global optima
Backtracking: It tries for all possible combinations to obtain solutions but will stop in the intermediate strategies
 if it can detect it cannot proceed further for yielding solution. eg., keeping n queens in n x n chess 
 board with constrain no queen are in same row or same column or diagonal. 
Dynamic programming: Principle of optimality and memoization. ie., outputs of previous steps are used in
future steps
Branch and bound: Branch - finding all possible solutions to  a subproblem, bound - ignoring solution if
the current soln is worse than optimal solution


Recursion:
void Print(int n) {
	if(n==0) {
		return 0;
	}
	System.out.println(n + “ “);
	return Print(n-1);
}
Each recursive call holds memory of function
It terminates when the base class is reached
If we get infinite recursion, the program may run out of memory and gives stack overflow
Generally iterative solutions are easier and efficient than recursive call because of overhead of memory in each function call